</USER_REQUEST>

<GROUND_TRUTH>
The pressure drop (ΔP) in a packed bed is influenced by the Reynolds number (NRe) as follows:  
- **At low Reynolds numbers (NRe ≤ 1)**: Viscous forces dominate, and ΔP is proportional to the fluid viscosity (μ) and the superficial velocity (Vs). This is described by the Kozeny–Karman equation, where ΔP ∝ μVs.  
- **At high Reynolds numbers (NRe ≥ 10,000)**: Inertial forces dominate, and ΔP is proportional to the fluid density (ρ) and the square of the superficial velocity (Vs²). This is described by the Burke–Plummer equation, where ΔP ∝ ρVs².  

Thus, the relationship between ΔP and NRe transitions from linear dependence on viscosity and velocity at low NRe to quadratic dependence on velocity and density at high NRe.  

\boxed{
\begin{aligned}
&\text{Low } N_{Re}: \Delta P \propto \mu V_s \\
&\text{High } N_{Re}: \Delta P \propto \rho V_s^2
\end{aligned}
}
</GROUND_TRUTH>

