The probability is given by $P(X = x_i)$ for $i = 1, 2, \ldots$.
We have $\sum_{i} P(x_i) = 1$.
We are given that $\mathbb{E}(X^2) = 0$.
We have $\mathbb{E}(X^2) = \sum_{i} x_i^2 P(x_i) = 0$.
Since $x_i^2 \geq 0$ and $P(x_i) \geq 0$ for all $i$, we must have $x_i^2 P(x_i) = 0$ for all $i$.
This means that for each $i$, either $x_i = 0$ or $P(x_i) = 0$.
If $x_i \neq 0$, then $P(x_i) = 0$.
Since $\sum_{i} P(x_i) = 1$, we must have $P(x_i) = 0$ for all $x_i \neq 0$.
Thus, the only possible value for $x_i$ is $x_i = 0$.
Therefore, $x_i = 0$ for all $i$.
Then $\sum_{i} P(x_i) = P(x_1) + P(x_2) + \cdots = P(0) + 0 + 0 + \cdots = 1$.
