","(a) $\int_{ -1}^{1} 2^{x} dx$
We know that $\int a^x dx = \frac{a^x}{\ln a} + C$.
So, $\int_{ -1}^{1} 2^{x} dx = \left[ \frac{2^x}{ \ln 2} \right]_{ -1}^{1} = \frac{2^1}{ \ln 2} - \frac{2^{-1}}{ \ln 2} = \frac{2}{ \ln 2} - \frac{1}{2 \ln 2} = \frac{4 - 1}{2 \ln 2} = \frac{3}{2 \ln 2}$.

(b) $\int_{0}^{1} (3e^x + 2\sqrt{x}) dx$
We have $\int (3e^x + 2\sqrt{x}) dx = 3 \int e^x dx + 2 \int x^{1/2} dx = 3e^x + 2 \frac{x^{3/2}}{3/2} + C = 3e^x + \frac{4}{3} x^{3/2} + C$.
So, $\int_{0}^{1} (3e^x + 2\sqrt{x}) dx = \left[ 3e^x + \frac{4}{3} x^{3/2} \right]_{0}^{1} = \left( 3e^1 + \frac{4}{3} (1)^{3/2} \right) - \left( 3e^0 + \frac{4}{3} (0)^{3/2} \right) = 3e + \frac{4}{3} - (3 + 0) = 3e + \frac{4}{3} - 3 = 3e - \frac{5}{3}$.

(c) $\int_{0}^{1} 2^{2y} dy$
Let $u = 2y$, so $dy = \frac{1}{2} du$. When $y = 0$, $u = 0$. When $y = 1$, $u = 2$.
Then $\int_{0}^{1} 2^{2y} dy = \int_{0}^{2} 2^u \frac{1}{2} du = \frac{1}{2} \int_{0}^{2} 2^u dy = \frac{1}{2} \left[ \frac{2^u}{ \ln 2} \right]_{0}^{2} = \frac{1}{2} \left( \frac{2^2}{ \ln 2} - \frac{.2^0}{ \ln 2} \right) = \frac{1}{2} \left( \frac{4}{ \ln 2} - \frac{1}{ \ln 2} \right) = \frac{1}{2} \left( \frac{3}{ \ln 2} \right) = \frac{3}{2 \ln 2}$.

