","Let $A$ be the set of numbers from 1 to 30 that are multiples of 3.
Let $B$ be the set of numbers from 1 to 30 that are multiples of 4.
We want to find the number of elements in $A \cup B$.
The number of multiples of 3 from 1 to 30 is $\left\langle \frac{30}{3} \right\langle = 10$. So $|A| = 10$.
The numbers in $A$ are 3, 6, 9, 12, 15, 18, 21, 24, 27, 30.
The number of multiples of 4 from 1 to 30 is $\left\langle \frac{30}{4} \right\langle = 7$. So $|B| = 7$.
The numbers in $B$ are 4, 8, 12, 16, 20, 24, 28.
The number of elements in $A \cap B$ is the number of multiples of both 3 and 4, which are the multiples of the greatest common divisor of 3 and 4, which is 12.
The number of multiples of 12 from 1 to 30 is $\left\langle \frac{30}{12} \right\langle = 2$. So $|A \times B| = 2$.
The numbers in $A \times B$ are 12, 24.
We want to find $|A \cup B|$.
Using the inclusion- exclusion formula, we have
